Which again is fairly interesting,

a) that it takes somewhere over 1000 games in the
string to get to my "56% chance of seeing five
spellbookless Oracle levels in a row" not the 680
games I thought would do that, and

b) that you have to play a string of around 5800
games to have 99::1 odds of having seen five
spellbookless Oracle levels in a row, so there
_will_ be (in absolute numbers, lots of) people in a
large enough group, like the rgrn readership, who've
never seen this happen and who perhaps are reluctant
to believe that it _does_ happen,

c) (of course) that even a 10,000 game string isn't
a _guarantee_ of having encountered the unusual
event, though it's getting a lot closer to being
one.

[Remembering that in all cases there have to be
four more games in the string than "tries" used as
an exponent in the calculation.]

